{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Find the differences between consecutive terms",
   "If constant: it is arithmetic. nth term = dn + (first term - d)",
   "If differences of differences are constant: it may be quadratic",
   "For geometric: find the common ratio r. nth term = ar^(n-1)"
  ],
  "title": "How to Find the nth Term of a Sequence",
  "content": "<p><strong>Arithmetic (linear) sequences</strong> have a constant difference \\(d\\) between consecutive terms. The nth term is \\(a + (n-1)d\\), or equivalently \\(dn + (a - d)\\), where \\(a\\) is the first term.</p><p><strong>Finding the nth term:</strong> 1) Find the common difference \\(d\\). 2) The nth term starts \\(dn + ...\\). 3) Find the zero term: subtract \\(d\\) from the first term. The nth term is \\(dn + \\text{(zero term)}\\).</p><p><strong>Geometric sequences</strong> have a constant ratio \\(r\\) between terms. The nth term is \\(ar^{n-1}\\).</p><p><strong>Special sequences:</strong> Square numbers (1, 4, 9, 16, ...), cube numbers (1, 8, 27, ...), triangular numbers (1, 3, 6, 10, ...), Fibonacci (each term = sum of two previous).</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Find the nth term of</strong> 5, 8, 11, 14, ...</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Common difference \\(d = 3\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> nth term = \\(3n + ...\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Zero term = \\(5 - 3 = 2\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(3n + 2\\)</p><p><strong>Check:</strong> When \\(n = 1\\): \\(3(1) + 2 = 5\\) \\(\\checkmark\\)</p>"
 },
